import{J as e,n as t,ot as n,q as r}from"./chunks/framework.NDkVE8yV.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"One entity model, with players and items in the same store","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"One entity model, with players and items in the same store"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":""}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0008-one-entity-model"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://jsprismarine.dev/adr/0008-one-entity-model"}]],"editSource":{"kind":"file","url":"https://github.com/JSPrismarine/JSPrismarine/edit/master/docs/adr/0008-one-entity-model.md"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0008-one-entity-model.md","filePath":"adr/0008-one-entity-model.md","lastUpdated":1794466183000}`),a={name:`adr/0008-one-entity-model.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<div><h1 id="one-entity-model-with-players-and-items-in-the-same-store" tabindex="-1">One entity model, with players and items in the same store <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-entity-model-with-players-and-items-in-the-same-store" aria-label="Permalink to “One entity model, with players and items in the same store”">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted</p><p>Every entity in a world lives in one entity store, and every entity has the shape a data-driven mob has today: a reference to a resolved entity type, the component state of an <code>EntityInstance</code>, and a body. The legacy class tree (<code>Entity</code>, <code>GenericEntity</code>, <code>Human</code>, <code>Player</code>, <code>Item</code> and <code>EyeOfEnderSignal</code>) and the mirror that copies each data-driven mob into a legacy body are removed. The store lives in Prismarine&#39;s host layer, because <code>@jsprismarine/entities</code> is documented as knowing nothing about a server. Items and the eye of ender, which Mojang ships no entity file for, get built-in definitions. The player&#39;s shipped definition leaves out what Bedrock keeps in code, such as <code>minecraft:health</code>, so the player gets a built-in overlay that adds only those parts and leaves the shipped components to the pack.</p><p>A player is an entity with player components for vitals, inventory, game mode and abilities. Its network session is a separate object outside the world. The session holds its entity&#39;s id, and the entity holds no reference to the session. Packets reach the entity as inputs addressed to that id, and the entity&#39;s changes reach the session through replication, which finds a player&#39;s session by entity id. Chat fan-out, permissions and persistence become services keyed by player identity. Respawn search becomes a level service, and a respawn into another dimension is a transfer between worlds.</p><p>Three things that are true of every mob are not true of a player, and the store states each as a rule. The client owns a player&#39;s movement, so the tick applies no gravity, navigation or pushes to that body, and an impulse aimed at it is an occurrence that replication sends to its client. A player that dies stays in the store and is revived by respawn, so its session&#39;s id keeps resolving. A player&#39;s health comes from the overlay above.</p><p>The direction of the session link was first recorded the other way round. <a href="/pr-preview/pr-2948/research/player-entity-spike.html">The player spike</a> counted the calls. The simulation reaches a session on 104 lines, and replication or a transfer message can serve every one of them, while every inbound packet has to reach the entity.</p><p>Every data-driven mob exists twice today, as an <code>EntityRuntime</code> entity and as a <code>GenericEntity</code> body in the world&#39;s legacy map, and health crosses between the two by comparing the body&#39;s value with the last one the mirror wrote. Two models mean two sets of rules for damage, tracking and replication. The project&#39;s first goal is to behave like Minecraft while following Mojang through data, and the data-driven model is the one that follows Mojang. Performance is a close second, and one model removes both the copy and the per-tick comparison. The cleanup lands as one program, so no slice ends with players on one model and mobs on the other.</p><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to “Considered options”">​</a></h2><p>A full ECS. The server re-architecture plan rejected it as the whole architecture because session and NBT data is heterogeneous, and it keeps structure-of-arrays storage as a later option inside the runtime. Parallel regions inside one world would need that storage. This program does not parallelize a world. It keeps the seams that make that possible later.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><p>The <code>Entity</code>, <code>Player</code>, <code>World</code> and <code>Item</code> classes stop being promised to plugin authors. The Bedrock Script API is the primary extension model, so the legacy plugin events are not a compatibility constraint on this change. Below 1.0.0, the versioning policy treats the removal as a <code>minor</code> bump.</p></div>`,1)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};